WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.630
If you suffer from severe obesity and have
been unable to lose weight, your doctor may

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:29.150
recommend robotic bariatric surgery.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:41.550
Your digestive system or gastrointestinal
tract includes your mouth, esophagus, stomach,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:48.650
small intestine, and large intestine.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:59.470
Your pancreas, liver, and gall bladder squirt
digestive juices into your small intestine

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.410
to help break down the food you eat.

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:09.150
Your doctor may recommend bariatric surgery
if you are unable to lose weight and keep

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:16.530
it off through diet and exercise alone, have
a body mass index or BMI above 40, have a

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:23.170
BMI above 35 and you have a life-threatening
condition such as heart disease or diabetes,

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:29.850
or weigh more than 100 pounds over your ideal
body weight or IBW.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:37.640
Bariatric surgery helps you lose weight by
decreasing the size of your stomach so that

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:43.530
it holds less food and makes you feel full
more quickly.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.690
Your procedure may include bypassing some
of your small intestine to decrease the length

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:54.130
of the path of food through your digestive
system as well as the amount of calories absorbed

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:59.870
from the food.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:05.630
Before your procedure an intravenous line
or IV will be started.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.790
You may be given antibiotics through the IV
to decrease your chance of infection.

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:14.260
You’ll be give general anesthesia.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.830
A breathing tube will be inserted through
your mouth and down your throat to help you

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:25.260
breathe during the operation.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:30.630
Your surgeon will make a small incision near
your belly button and insert a plastic tube

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.510
called a port.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:37.900
Carbon dioxide gas will be pumped into your
abdomen through this port.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:43.700
The gas will inflate the abdomen, giving your
surgeon more room to see and move the surgical

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.650
tools.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:53.890
After your abdomen is inflated, a high-definition
camera will be inserted into this port.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:59.260
Your surgeon will make additional port incisions
for robotic instruments as well as instruments

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.880
used by patient-side assistants.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:09.050
An assistant will insert all of the robotic
tools through these ports.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:15.250
Unlike standard laparoscopic instruments,
these tools can rotate 360-degrees and have

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:20.770
more flexibility than the human wrist.

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:26.680
Seated at a special console, your surgeon
will operate the robotic arms and the camera

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.290
with joystick-like controls and foot pedals.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:37.260
A computer will translate the exact movements
of your surgeon’s fingers into precise movements

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.489
of the surgical tools.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:46.510
At the same time, a high-definition vision
system will provide a magnified, three-dimensional,

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:51.350
stereoscopic view of the surgical area.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:04:02.200
Two common robotic bariatric procedures are
adjustable gastric banding and gastric bypass.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.849
If you are having an adjustable gastric banding
procedure, your surgeon will apply a restrictive

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:14.269
band around your stomach to limit the amount
of food your stomach can hold.

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:19.849
Tubing will connect the band to a port just
beneath the skin of your abdomen.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:25.229
Your surgeon will inject saline solution into
the tubing to adjust the band’s tightness

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:33.400
as needed to hasten your weight loss or reduce
side effects.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.719
If you are having a gastric bypass procedure,
your surgeon will create a small pouch in

00:04:38.719 --> 00:04:45.190
your stomach and separate the rest of your
stomach and upper section of your small intestine.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:53.180
The lower section of your small intestine
will be attached to the stomach pouch.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.960
Then your surgeon will reattach the upper
section of your small intestine to a different

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.759
part of your lower small intestine which will
allow digestive juices from your pancreas,

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:09.370
liver, and gall bladder to help digest your
food.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:15.240
At the end of your procedure, the incision
will be closed with stitches, staples, surgical

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.710
glue, or closure tape dressings.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:25.139
After your procedure, your breathing tube
will be removed, and you will be taken to

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:27.570
the recovery area for monitoring.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:31.699
You will be given pain medication as needed.

00:05:31.699 --> 00:05:35.919
You will continue to receive antibiotics through
you IV.

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:40.929
Most patients are released from the hospital
one to two days after the procedure.

